ally required to confirm MGZL. This elevates the diagnostic process itself (not just the disease) to a key entity in the community, reflecting that MGZL’s overlap with related lymphomas can make classification challenging. [Data: Entities (4926, 4952); Relationships (10898)]\\n\\nAn expert hematopathologist is directly linked to diagnosis confirmation, with expert review described as essential for confirming MGZL. This relationship indicates that specialized pathology expertise is treated as a critical capability for accurate identification within this community. [Data: Entities (4951, 4952, 4926); Relationships (10924, 10898)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Microscopic overlap is represented via Reed–Sternberg(-like) cells and multinucleation\",\"explanation\":\"Reed–Sternberg cells are described as abnormally large lymphocytes that often contain more than one nucleus and are characteristic of classical Hodgkin lymphoma. The dataset further states that MGZL cells may resemble Reed–Sternberg cells, capturing a key element of histologic overlap that can complicate differential diagnosis across Hodgkin and related mediastinal entities. [Data: Entities (1224, 4945, 4943, 4926); Relationships (1997, 10887)]\\n\\nThe nucleus entity is connected through the description that Reed–Sternberg cells may have more than one nucleus, emphasizing multinucleation as a recognizable microscopic feature. This provides a concrete morphologic bridge in the community between cellular descriptors and lymphoma classification. [Data: Entities (1231, 1224); Relationships (1997)]\\n\\nHodgkin lymphoma is also linked to Reed–Sternberg cells as Hodgkin-like cells, reinforcing that Reed–Sternberg morphology is a core reference point for Hodgkin-related diagnosis and for explaining similarities in gray zone entities. [Data: Entities (4928, 4945); Relationships (10907)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Gray zone lymphomas include mediastinal and non-mediastinal types, with non-mediastinal presented as the other main type\",\